#
# Lenovo Tab Pen Plus — the stylus's *Bluetooth* side. Not to be confused with
# the drawing digitizer, which is a separate SPI device (`NVTCapacitivePen`,
# VID/PID 0000:0000, INPUT_PROP_DIRECT) that needs no configuration at all.
#
# 17ef:617f enumerates over BT HID as two nodes (measured with `getevent -i`
# on the running stock ROM):
#
#   "Lenovo Tab Pen Plus Consumer Control"  EV=13  KEY=1<<48 (KEY_UNKNOWN=240)
#                                                  MSC=MSC_SCAN
#   "Lenovo Tab Pen Plus Mouse"             EV=17  KEY=BTN_LEFT/RIGHT/MIDDLE
#                                                  REL=X,Y,WHEEL,HWHEEL
#
# The side-button gestures live on the Consumer Control node. Their HID usages
# (consumer page 0x0c, 0x600-0x605) are Lenovo-private, so the kernel HID map
# has no keycode for them and emits KEY_UNKNOWN plus the raw usage in MSC_SCAN.
# That is exactly what the `key usage <code>` form is for — KeyboardInputMapper
# falls back to the usage code when the scan code maps to nothing.
#
# ⚠️ The stock file cannot be copied verbatim. It maps all six usages to Lenovo
# labels — PEN_ONE_CLICK, PEN_TWO_CLICK, PEN_THREE_CLICK, PEN_LONG_CLICK,
# PEN_PRESS_CLICK, PEN_BT_DISCONNECT — none of which exist in AOSP, and
# KeyLayoutMap::load() returns BAD_VALUE and discards the ENTIRE file on the
# first unknown label. One bad label would kill every mapping below it.
#
# ★★ ALL SIX ARE DELIBERATELY LEFT UNMAPPED. Do not "improve" this by mapping
# them to STYLUS_BUTTON_PRIMARY / _SECONDARY / _TERTIARY / _TAIL. That is what
# this file used to do, and it broke tapping with the pen. The chain, every link
# confirmed on the running device:
#
#   1. EventHub.cpp:2601-2607 — a device that is not TOUCH/TOUCH_MT, is not
#      ALPHAKEY, and has ANY keycode from STYLUS_BUTTON_KEYCODES
#      (KeyCodeClassifications.h:48-53, which is exactly those four) is
#      classified EXTERNAL_STYLUS. The Consumer Control node matches all three
#      tests. Measured: `Classes: KEYBOARD | EXTERNAL_STYLUS | EXTERNAL`.
#   2. That creates an ExternalStylusInputMapper (InputDevice.cpp:622-625) and
#      flips BOTH Novatek nodes to `ExternalStylusPresence: BUTTON_FUSION`
#      (TouchInputMapper::resolveExternalStylusPresence, :338-354). Measured on
#      the touchscreen and the pen.
#   3. TouchButtonAccumulator::processMappedKey (:118-131) returns early when
#      mapKey() fails and never clears mBtnStylus, so the button state LATCHES.
#      Measured: `External Stylus State: Button State: 0x00000020` — a barrel
#      button reported as held for ~50 minutes while nobody was touching the
#      device.
#   4. applyExternalStylusButtonState() ORs that bit into every touch frame, and
#      the dispatch order at TouchInputMapper.cpp:1487-1491 emits
#      ACTION_BUTTON_RELEASE immediately before ACTION_UP.
#   5. View.java:17011-17015 sets mIgnoreNextUpEvent on ACTION_BUTTON_RELEASE,
#      and :18323 then skips performClickInternal(). ACTION_MOVE is untouched.
#
#   Net effect, and it matches the owner's report word for word: dragging works,
#   tapping intermittently does nothing.
#
# There is no functional loss from leaving these unmapped. The pen's two real
# barrel buttons are reported by the DIGITIZER, not over Bluetooth, as
# BTN_STYLUS / BTN_STYLUS2, and Generic.kl:307-308 already maps those to
# STYLUS_BUTTON_PRIMARY / _SECONDARY. The digitizer node is TOUCH-classified so
# it is exempt from the rule in step 1.
#
# What the six usages actually are, and why none of them has an honest AOSP
# keycode — an unmapped usage is simply ignored, which is the correct behaviour
# for a gesture Android has no concept of:
#   PEN_ONE_CLICK     — a click of the BT button, not a barrel-button press;
#                       stock routes it to PenService's radial menu.
#   PEN_TWO_CLICK     — double click, same.
#   PEN_THREE_CLICK   — STYLUS_BUTTON_TAIL means the eraser end, not a 3rd click.
#   PEN_LONG_CLICK    — press-and-hold; belongs in a gesture layer.
#   PEN_PRESS_CLICK   — same.
#   PEN_BT_DISCONNECT — a pairing-state signal, not an input event at all.
#
# Stock reaches the same end state by accident rather than on purpose: its file
# uses the six Lenovo-private labels, KeyLayoutMap::load() rejects the first one
# and discards the whole file, so on stock this node has no layout either.
#
# The file itself must stay — device.mk copies it by name — and an empty
# keylayout is valid.
#
# The stock `.idc` for this device is deliberately NOT carried over. It sets
# `touch.orientationAware` and `touch.deviceType = touchScreen`, and both keys
# are read only by TouchInputMapper (TouchInputMapper.cpp:362 and its
# configureParameters). Neither 617f node has any absolute axis, so
# TouchInputMapper is never instantiated for them — the Mouse node is a relative
# pointer and goes to CursorInputMapper, which reads `cursor.orientationAware`
# instead. The stock file is inert on this hardware; carrying it would only add
# a config whose keys nothing reads.

# Intentionally no mappings. See above.
